A||||9||Philphine||irm egr tube worth it?||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||09-13-2001||06:47 PM||||nothing new under the sun. the vaccume leak on my '86 gt turned out to be the egr tube broken under the insulation. read old threads and saw the irm tube mentioned but no one who has it. dose it seem worth getting. do i still need some kind of insulation around it if i get one.<P>also, i just took it off before i came to work so i haven't priced a normal one. what do they go for for a stock one? thanks for any help. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black86SE||09-13-2001||08:02 PM||||My car doesn't have the egr tube on it but I have thought about getting a new one. The Fiero Store has one for about $95, I found another place that has them for about $75, and <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  has them for about $60 inc. shipping. GM Parts Direct only use part numbers of vehicles before '94. Do another search for egr part numbers or something and copy and paste it there and it will tell you. There are two different part numbers and I think two different prices. I tried to make my own egr tube out of 5/8" soft copper but when I put it on my manifolds got red hot. So I took it back off, blocked the two ports again and it still did it. Then I noticed that I didn't put the big vac. line back into the throttle body. Then it didn't do it anymore but I am not gonna put that egr back on for a while (pain in the ass). Hope this helps.<P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000002||Philphine||09-13-2001||08:31 PM||||hmmm....not much price difference from the irm one ($90 in the catalog i looked at). it's supposed to last longer 'cause it's more flexable. guess if i don't run across a used one i'll try to get it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rick 88||09-13-2001||08:39 PM||||The new GM piece I got was much better material than the original tube. It also had the insulation pipe like the original. Odds are a junkyard tube will be the old style. My old tube came off in two pieces yet it barely leaked.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><P>Rick<BR>yellow 88 GT 5-speed||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black86SE||09-13-2001||09:22 PM||||yeah, I took one from a car in a junkyard but it was cracked at the flange. It was the old style. I still took it so that I could use the flange when I made my own.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero_2.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000005||cifc||09-13-2001||09:53 PM||||The IRM tube is worth it.  I've had one on for about 4 years now.  The one I had before it I got from GM and it only lasted a month.  <BR>--Jason||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||09-14-2001||06:57 PM||||I had a 1/2" long, circumferential crack about 1" up from the EGR valve itself, inside of the corrugations.  Instead of spending the $80 my dealer wanted for the pipe kit, I tried some exhaust patch filler from Pep Boys.  Inferno Metal Repair, good to 2000 degrees according to the packaging, about $5 for a small bottle.  One part, no mixing.  I filled in the cracked section, then put a small stainless hose clamp around it before it dried (kind of like rebar in concrete, I figured), then put more filler on top of that.  I then [put the tube back on and made sure the cracked area was still well covered before it dried.  Air dry 3-4 hours, then heat cure slowly (I started it up and let it idle for about 5 minutes, then let it sit overnight.  <BR>So far, it's gone 3 weeks and no problems.<p>[This message has been edited by 2birds (edited 09-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black86SE||09-15-2001||12:14 AM||||Well how hot does the EGR tube actually get? I have made my own out of 5/8" copper but havent used it yet. I am thinking about using solder fittings but am worried about the heat.<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/myfiero_3.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>1986 V6 SE<BR>Black<BR>17" Wheels<BR>P235/45/ZR17 <BR>BF Goodrich g-Force KDWS||||reg||1
Z||000008||seadog||09-15-2001||06:47 AM||||Yes the IRM one is worth it, I've had mine a couple of years now. They are tuff and well made.  ||||reg||14
Z||000009||2birds||09-15-2001||11:03 PM||||Sorry, but in my previous post I said the Inferno Metal repair was good to 200 degrees.  Well, I got lazy on the zero key.  It's good to 2000 degrees.  Exhaust temps can get up close to 900-1000 degrees, from what I've read.  Ever notice your manifolds glowing red (especially with a vacuum leak making it run lean)?||||reg||1
